{"name":"Beauty Spot","hex":"#604938","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beauty spot","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thing (especially an aspect of something) which is beautiful.\n\n A natural mark on a person's skin, especially a freckle or mole on a woman's face.","A natural mark on a person's skin, especially a freckle or mole on a woman's face."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beauty_spot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beauty Spot","blocks":[{"text":"Beauty Spot is a 1932 French musical comedy film directed by Pierre Caron and Léonce Perret and starring Simone Cerdan, Marfa d'Hervilly and Doris Morrey. It is a remake of the 1931 German film Opera Ball and is part of the operetta film genre."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9b/Beauty_Spot_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty_Spot","wikidata":"Q16761575"},{"title":"Beauty mark","blocks":[{"text":"A beauty mark or beauty spot is a euphemism for a type of dark facial mark so named because such birthmarks are sometimes considered an attractive feature. Medically, such \"beauty marks\" are generally melanocytic nevus, more specifically the compound variant. Moles of this type may also be located elsewhere on the body, and may also be considered beauty marks if located on the face, shoulder, neck or breast. Artificial beauty marks have been fashionable in some periods."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/CindyCrawfordOct09.jpg/330px-CindyCrawfordOct09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty_mark","wikidata":"Q167078"},{"title":"Beauty","blocks":[{"text":"Beauty is commonly described as a feature of objects that makes them pleasurable to perceive. Such objects include landscapes, sunsets, humans and works of art. Beauty, art and taste are the main subjects of aesthetics, one of the fields of study within philosophy. As a positive aesthetic value, it is contrasted with ugliness as its negative counterpart."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg/330px-Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty","wikidata":"Q7242"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg/500px-Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg/500px-Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Allegorical Painting of Two Ladies, English School, c. 1650, by an anonymous hand. The two women, who appear to be of equal standing, are wearing face patches, which were a fashion of the time. The painting is captioned \"I black with white bespott y white with blacke this evil proceeds from thy proud hart then take her: Devill.\"[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnon_(English_School)_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Ri